menu "DeskEmoji robot"

config ROBOT_ENABLE_ESP_SR
    bool "Enable offline ESP-SR wake word"
    default y


config ROBOT_ENABLE_GESTURE
    bool "Enable PAJ7620U2 gestures"
    default y

config ROBOT_ENABLE_RANDOM_ANIMATION
    bool "Enable idle random animation"
    default y

config ROBOT_ENABLE_SERVO
    bool "Enable dual servos"
    default y


config ROBOT_SERVO_X_PIN
    int "Horizontal servo GPIO"
    range 0 48
    default 11
    help
        Signal pin of the horizontal (pan) servo.  Default 11 matches the
        DeskEmoji board this firmware was ported from; change it when a board
        variant wires its servos differently.

config ROBOT_SERVO_Y_PIN
    int "Vertical servo GPIO"
    range 0 48
    default 12
    help
        Signal pin of the vertical (tilt) servo.  Default 12, same caveat as
        the horizontal pin.

config ROBOT_SERVO_SELFTEST
    bool "Sweep both servos once at boot (bring-up aid)"
    default n
    help
        Runs before Wi-Fi comes up and sweeps the horizontal then the vertical
        servo from limit to limit, logging each phase.  Useful to tell a silent
        servo apart from a firmware or connection problem: if nothing moves here,
        the fault is on the hardware side (power, wiring, servo).  Adds a few
        seconds to boot, so keep it off for normal use.

config ROBOT_MIC_RIGHT_CHANNEL
    bool "I2S microphone drives the RIGHT channel"
    default n
    help
        The L/R pin of the microphone selects which half of the I2S frame it
        drives: tied to GND it drives the left half, tied to VDD the right one.
        This firmware reads the left half, which matches the DeskEmoji board
        and the schematic.  Reading the half the microphone does not drive
        yields nothing but silence - which looks exactly like a dead microphone
        except that the captured level is precisely zero everywhere.

        Enable this only if the microphone is wired the other way round.
        Check pin 6 (L/R) of the microphone against GND with a multimeter
        first: continuity to GND means the default is already correct.

config ROBOT_MIC_SAMPLE_RATE
    int "Microphone sample rate (Hz)"
    range 8000 48000
    default 16000
    help
        Rate of the capture channel only.  The speaker keeps its own fixed rate
        because the server sends 16 kHz speech back and both ends have to agree;
        capture and playback are separate I2S controllers, so this one can move
        on its own.

        The board this firmware was ported from runs its I2S microphone at
        24000.  16000 is also inside the microphone's supported clock range and
        is what the cloud speech service expects to be told, so the default
        stays there.  Change it only when investigating capture problems, and
        remember the device reports this value to the server in the
        X-Sample-Rate header, so the two cannot drift apart.

config ROBOT_MIC_SHIFT
    int "Microphone 32-bit to 16-bit right shift (capture gain)"
    range 8 16
    default 16
    help
        The I2S peripheral hands over one 32-bit slot per sample while the
        microphone drives only its top bits, so the raw value is shifted right
        before it is clamped into 16 bits.  This shift is the capture gain, and
        it is the one number that decides whether the audio is usable:

          too small - ordinary speech lands past full scale, the clamp flattens
                      it into a square wave, and recognition hears nothing while
                      the level still looks healthy: the peak sits pinned at
                      32768, the RMS is over half of full scale, and nearly every
                      sample counts as "voiced"
          too large - the audio is quiet and recognition struggles the other way

        This microphone puts its 24 bits in the top of the 32-bit slot, so 16 is
        the value that maps its full range onto the full 16-bit range.  The
        firmware this board was ported from uses 12 and still works, because it
        feeds the microphone through an audio front end with automatic gain
        control; this firmware sends the raw capture to the server and so has to
        get the gain right here.

        Measured on the DeskEmoji board at 24000 Hz: with 12, speech reports a
        peak of 32768, an RMS of 18552 and 14-16% of samples clamped.  Both the
        bit alignment and that measurement point at 16, so it is the default.
        Only lower it if the captured audio turns out too quiet.


config ROBOT_STA_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
    int "Saved Wi-Fi connection timeout"
    range 10 180
    default 60

endmenu
